          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "MED",
            "location": "packages/project-memory/src/ownership.ts:1001",
            "message": "acquireOwnership fans out to 7 distinct callees (loadNative, errno, classifyLayout, assertFile, checkIdentity, initializeLayout, closeDescriptor) — far more than any other function in the changed set. It mixes native-lock acquisition, layout branching (new vs existing), two different validation paths, and fd cleanup in one routine, making it hard to reason about which cleanup/error path runs for each branch. Consider splitting the new-layout and existing-layout branches into separate helpers so each path's error handling is locally verifiable.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          },
          {
            "dimension": "quality",
            "severity": "MED",
            "location": "packages/project-memory/src/host-node.ts:1",
            "message": "host-node.ts calls prepareRoot(options) and then acquireOwnership(root, ...) as two separate top-level steps, but both independently call classifyLayout on the same root. Between the two calls another process could initialize the layout (classifyLayout is also reached from ownership.test.ts's initializeLayout/acquireOwnership paths), so the 'new' vs 'existing' decision prepareRoot's caller relies on can disagree with the one acquireOwnership re-derives moments later, and the redundant stat/metadata read is also wasted work on the happy path.",
            "confidence": "observation"
          }
        ]
      },
